          %h3#InfinitesimalThickening Infinitesimal thickening and formal smoothness
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cahiers+topos"} Cahiers topos
          .num_example
            %h6#example_7 Example
            %p
              Consider a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              inclusion
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Disc
                      %mi 𝒫
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Disc^{\mathcal{P}} \;\colon\; \mathcal{S} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{P}
            %p
              which has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                      %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0^{\mathcal{P}}
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma^{\mathcal{P}}
              that coincide with each other
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                      %mi 𝒫
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi 𝒫
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Pi_0^{\mathcal{P}} \simeq \Gamma^{\mathcal{P}}
                    \\,.
            %p
              In (
              %a{:href => "#LawvereAxiomatic"}> Lawvere 07, def. 1
              ) this situation is said to exhibit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              as a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quality+type"} quality type
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒮
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            %p
              It follows that there is an infinite sequence of adjoints, in particular that there is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi coDisc
                      %mi 𝒫
                    %mo ≔
                    %msup
                      %mi Disc
                      %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} coDisc^{\mathcal{P}} \coloneqq Disc^{\mathcal{P}}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Γ
                        %mi 𝒫
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma^{\mathcal{P}}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
              is also a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                      %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0^{\mathcal{P}}
              preserves
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
              (in fact all
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            %p
              So the above adjoints makes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
              be a cohesive topos over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"} base topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              with the special property that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 0
                        %mi 𝒫
                      %mo ≃
                      %msup
                        %mi Γ
                        %mi 𝒫
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0^{\mathcal{P}} \simeq \Gamma^{\mathcal{P}}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
              every cohesive neighbourhood contains precisely one point. This is a characteristic of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+points"} infinitesimally thickened points
            %p
              See at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+cohesion"} infinitesimal cohesion
              for more on this.
          %h3#CounterExamples Counter-examples
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong Counter-Example
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              be a non-trivial
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo •
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G = \{\bullet \stackrel{g}{\to} \bullet | g \in G\}
              for its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+topos"} presheaf topos
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi G
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    PSh(\mathbf{B}G) \simeq G Set
            %p
              is the category of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation+representation"} permutation representation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Const
                    %mo ⊣
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mo ←
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mi Set
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %munder
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %munder
                              %mi lim
                              %mo ←
                        %mover
                          %mo ←
                          %mi Const
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %munder
                            %mi lim
                            %mo →
                    %mi Set
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\Pi_0 \dashv Const \dashv \lim_\leftarrow) : G Set
                    \\stackrel{\overset{\lim_\to}{\to}}{\stackrel{\overset{Const}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{\lim_\leftarrow}{\to}}}
                    Set
                    \\,.
            %p
              The colimit over a representation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, \rho) : \mathbf{B}G \to Set
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
              set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V/\rho(G)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Pi_0(G) \simeq *
            %p but
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mover
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Pi_0(G \times G) \simeq \bar n
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              denotes the fundamental representation of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              on itself. Therefore
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0
              does not preserve products in this case.
            %p
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+topos"} locally connected topos
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+%E2%88%9E-connected+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} locally ∞-connected (∞,1)-topos
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topos"} connected topos
                    \/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-connected+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} ∞-connected (∞,1)-topos
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strongly+connected+topos"} strongly connected topos
                    \/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strongly+%E2%88%9E-connected+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} strongly ∞-connected (∞,1)-topos
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+connected+topos"} totally connected topos
                    \/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+%E2%88%9E-connected+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} totally ∞-connected (∞,1)-topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+topos"} local topos
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} local (∞,1)-topos
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pi+modality"} Pi modality
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊣
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊣
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
            %li
              %p
                %strong cohesive topos
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+site"}> locally connected site
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+%E2%88%9E-connected+site"} locally ∞-connected site
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+site"} connected site
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+site"} local site
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+site"} cohesive site
          %h2#References References
          %p The axioms for a cohesive topos originate around
          %ul
            %li#LawvereCatsOfSpaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categories of spaces may not be generalized spaces, as exemplified by directed graphs
              %strong XX
              (1986) 179-186, reprinted as: Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories,
              %strong 9
              (2005) 1-7 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/9/tr9abs.html"}> tac:tr9
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p where however the term “cohesive topos” was not yet used.
          %p This appears maybe first in
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere94
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohesive+Toposes+and+Cantor%27s+%22lauter+Einsen%22"} Cohesive Toposes and Cantor's "lauter Einsen"
              %strong 2
              1 (1994) 5-15 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/philmat/2.1.5"}> doi:10.1093/philmat/2.1.5
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LawvereCohesiveToposes.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p The term “cohesion” and parts of its later axiomatization (p. 245) appears thoughout section C.1 of
          %ul
            %li#LawvereRosebrugh03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Rosebrugh"} Robert Rosebrugh
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets+for+Mathematics"} Sets for Mathematics
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511755460"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511755460
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mta.ca/~rrosebru/setsformath/"} book homepage
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://patryshev.com/books/Sets%20for%20Mathematics.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Under the name
            %em categories of cohesion
            a formal axiomatization is given in
          %ul
            %li#LawvereAxiomatic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Axiomatic cohesion
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/19/3/19-03abs.html"} tac:19-03
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/19/3/19-03.pdf"} pdf
          %p (This does demand the conditions that “cohesive pieces have points” and that “pieces of powers are powers of pieces” as part of the definition of “category of cohesion”.)
          %p
            This builds on a series of precursors of attempts to identify axiomatics for
            = succeed "es." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gros+topos"} gros topos
          %p In
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Some+Thoughts+on+the+Future+of+Category+Theory"} Some Thoughts on the Future of Category Theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Como"} Como
              1991
          %p
            the term
            %em category of Being
            is used for a notion resembling that of a cohesive topos (with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+quadruple"} adjoint quadruple
            but not considering
            %em pieces have points
            or
            = succeed ")." do
              %em discrete objects are concrete
            = succeed "." do
              %em Becoming
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"}> Georg Hegel
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
              (see
              %em
                %a{:href => "#http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic#OnBeingAndBecoming"} On being and becoming
              ).
          %p and specifically the term “cohesion” probably from
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"}> Georg Hegel
              , part II “Philosophy of Nature”, second section “Physics”, B c) “cohesion”, in
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+the+Philosophical+Sciences"} Encyclopedia of the Philosophical Sciences
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              %em Categories of space and quantity
              in: J. Echeverria et al (eds.),
              %em The Space of mathematics
              , de Gruyter, Berlin, New York (1992)
          %p
            a proposal for a general axiomatization of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
          %p The left and right adjoint to the global section functor as a means to identify discrete spaces and codiscrete space is mentioned
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Taking categories seriously
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/8/tr8.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/8/tr8.pdf#page=14"} page 14
          %p
            The precise term
            %em cohesive topos
            apparently first appeared publically in the lecture
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohesive+Toposes+--+Combinatorial+and+Infinitesimal+Cases"} Cohesive Toposes – Combinatorial and Infinitesimal Cases
              Como (2008)
          %p
            Notes for these lectures are in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://comocategoryarchive.com/People/Lawvere/FWLawvere-Cohesive-Toposes-Como-January-2008.pdf"} this pdf
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Walters"} Bob Walters
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://comocategoryarchive.com/"} Como Category Theory Archive
          %p The notion of “cohesion” was explored earlier in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Volterra’s functionals and covariant cohesion of space
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.acsu.buffalo.edu/~wlawvere/Volterra.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            where (on p. 9) it is suggested that “almost any”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive category
            may be called a “species of cohesion”.
          %p An analysis of the interdependency of the axioms on a cohesive topos is in
          %ul
            %li#Johnstone11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Remarks on punctual local connectedness
              %strong 25
              3 (2011) 51-63 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/25/3/25-03abs.html"}> tac:25-03
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Discussion of “sufficient cohesion” is in
          %ul
            %li#Menni14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mat%C3%ADas+Menni"}> Matías Menni
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Continuous Cohesion over sets
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/29/20/29-20abs.html"} TAC
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://sites.google.com/site/matiasmenni/continuityOverSets12.pdf?attredirects=0"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mat%C3%ADas+Menni"}> Matías Menni
                ,
                %em Sufficient Cohesion over Atomic Toposes
                , Cah. Top.Géom. Diff. Cat.
                %strong LV
                (2014) (
                %a{:href => "https://sites.google.com/site/matiasmenni/SufCohesion12.pdf?attredirects=0"}> preprint
                )
          %p
            Discussion of relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation+topology"} double negation topology
            is in
          %ul
            %li#LawvereMenni15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mat%C3%ADas+Menni"} Matías Menni
              = succeed "," do
                %em Internal choice holds in the discrete part of any cohesive topos satisfying stable connected codiscreteness
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/30/26/30-26abs.html"} TAC
          %p
            And further relations of cohesion to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Birkhoff%27s+theorem"} Birkhoff's theorem
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal algebra
            is given in
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Birkhoff’s Theorem from a geometric perspective: A simple example
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://cgasa.sbu.ac.ir/article_12425.html"} journal page
          %p A good deal of the structure of cohesive toposes is also considered in
          %ul
            %li#KontsevichRosenbergSpaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Rosenberg"} Alexander Rosenberg
              = succeed "," do
                %em Noncommutative spaces
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/preprints/send?bid=2331"} ps
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/preprints/send?bid=2303"} dvi
          %p
            under the name
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Q-categories"} Q-categories
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+toposes"} local toposes
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#AwodeyBirkedal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+Birkedal"} Lars Birkedal
              = succeed "," do
                %em Elementary axioms for local maps of toposes
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://cs.au.dk/~birke/papers/elealm.ps.gz"} ps
          %p
            Notation and terminology above, and generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
            etc., follows:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"} differential cohomology in a cohesive topos
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.7930"}> arXiv:1310.7930
              )
          %p Exposition:
          %ul
            %li#Loregian19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fosco+Loregian"}> Fosco Loregian
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Cohesion in Rome
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://tetrapharmakon.github.io/stuff/cohesive_rome.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LoregianCohesion.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
